00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:34.750
So as you probably
know, the University

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:38.020
of Southern California is
a large private university

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.450
and academic medical
center located

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:42.980
in Los Angeles, California.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.000
We have strong research
programs in many clinical areas,

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.470
such as cancer, biomedical
imaging, neurosciences,

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.300
stem cells, and many more.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.310
And collectively,
our researchers

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:55.750
conduct at least 20--

00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:00.250
sorry, 200 clinical
trials per year.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:03.100
And those clinical
trials, the goal of them

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:08.950
are to basically better
understand diseases, as well as

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:13.610
create new therapies and new
standards of care for patients.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:15.910
So our Clinical and
Translational Sciences

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.010
Institute, where
I'm from, is charged

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:21.640
with improving and enhancing
the research pipeline

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:25.960
to get more treatments to
more patients more quickly.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.590
And given our local
population in LA,

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:32.860
we bring special expertise
in diverse populations,

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:36.460
engaging diverse people
in clinical research,

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:39.580
and we bring that expertise
to our larger national network

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:41.800
of institutes like ours.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:47.500
And that's important because,
when you test a treatment just

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.938
with one segment
of the population,

00:03:49.938 --> 00:03:51.730
you don't know how well
that treatment will

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:54.170
work for everybody else.

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:58.540
So we aim to correct that by
bringing in more Los Angelenos

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:03.010
into clinical research, and also
trying to enhance the research

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:06.670
efficiency for all studies.

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:12.370
So two years ago, we
surveyed researchers at USC.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:15.700
And many people complained
about different administrative

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:20.620
processes burdens, like too much
paperwork, too many systems,

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:25.550
long approval times and
unclear requirements.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:27.580
And it's true.

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:31.060
We were able to measure it,
and in the long lifecycle

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:34.960
of a single clinical
trial, from coming up

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.050
with the idea to
publishing the results,

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:41.510
there is a long
pause in the middle.

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:43.450
And it's not actual
science that's

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:45.210
going on during this pause.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:47.670
The pause is for
approvals and logistics.

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:50.500
They're important things, of
course, like ethics committees

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.620
to make sure that the
patients are undergoing

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.110
something that's safe.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:58.390
They're setting up
pharmacy orders,

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:01.240
and they're negotiating
contracts and budgets

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.120
with the pharmaceutical
companies or other sponsors who

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:05.930
are paying for the study.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:11.380
And so this activation process
within the red circles,

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:17.610
that takes about 10 to 20 people
at least 80 hours per study

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:20.770
to complete, with lots of
back-and-forth and long

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:22.370
response times.

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:24.370
So even though
it's 80 man hours,

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:27.260
it can take up to three
months, on average.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:30.710
And so that translates into a
delay in getting the treatments

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:31.210
to patients.

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:36.000
So we decided it's
time to change.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.790
We embarked on a process
improvement initiative

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:42.030
to make the entire activation
process faster and more

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:46.830
streamlined and more transparent
for the research teams at USC.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:52.290
Our scope was studies that
use hospital resources, where

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:54.540
the study-related
costs had to be

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.900
distinguished and separated
from the regular clinical care

00:05:57.900 --> 00:05:59.200
costs.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:03.330
And we decided to take a lean
process improvement approach.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.940
And to do that, we worked with
a partner in the USC Marshall

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.350
School of Business who runs the
Global Center for Supply Chain

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:11.610
Management.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:14.580
And our thinking there was that,
if his center could improve

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:17.490
quality and speed
in manufacturing,

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:20.080
it could probably
work for research.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:24.500
So we formed these working
groups and we got to work.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:28.110
And this was quite a culture
shift because, as you may know,

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:31.320
speed is not exactly
the number one priority

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.750
in academic administration.

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:37.010
But the exercise of
looking step by step

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:39.420
in everybody's
individual processes

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:41.760
compared to all the
other offices involved

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:43.860
in the whole activation
process, that

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:45.720
exercise of mapping
the workflow really

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:48.750
helped us see the
duplication that exists

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:50.010
and the redundancies.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:53.490
It helped us identify these
bottlenecks and the friction

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:58.180
points, which we termed
opportunities for improvement.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:01.350
And then we took a step back
and we tried to, together,

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:04.560
create an ideal
future state workflow,

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.270
and started to implement
those improvements.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.280
And this is too small
to read, of course,

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:11.730
but this diagram
should just give you

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:16.770
a sense of how complex the
research activation process is.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:20.340
And that's the
future state diagram.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:23.070
So fast forward a couple
years, by last year

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:26.370
we had implemented
over a quarter

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:29.340
of the steps, which is
a big reduction in waste

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:30.540
and duplication.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:32.910
And it was just small,
incremental improvements,

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:37.530
by cutting out a
step here and there

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:41.700
and introducing new processes.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:45.750
And we have made the majority
of the changes in real life,

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:49.410
and the outcome has been
faster approvals for studies.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:55.260
So the average has dropped from
about 80 to around 60 days.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:58.380
And we think another outcome is
a happier researcher community.

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:01.980
So we surveyed the
research community again,

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:04.050
and they said the
process has become

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:07.740
easier and less frustrating.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:09.900
But we know we have more
to do, so in the spirit

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.420
of continuous
improvement, we wanted

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:14.160
to keep the momentum going.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:15.690
And we knew we
had an opportunity

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:19.680
to really improve further
with exponential technology.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:23.730
So we turned to Google Cloud
and machine learning and AI.

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:26.820
And with the help
of Pluto7, we were

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:31.140
able to see the potential of
using those technologies in one

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:32.429
piece of the process.

00:08:32.429 --> 00:08:36.210
So using a proof of concept
project with Pluto7,

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:40.440
we applied ML and AI to the part
of the budgeting process that's

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:43.049
called Medicare
coverage analysis.

00:08:43.049 --> 00:08:47.310
And I'll now turn it over to our
MCA expert at USC, Joanne Pak.

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:56.440
JOANNE PAK: So MCA is
Medicare coverage analysis.

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:59.170
And so basically,
this is our workflow.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:02.050
This is not just for
USC, but this is usually

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:04.620
what happens in
other academic sites.

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:08.050
So Medicare coverage
analysis, basically what we do

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:13.000
is we look at the studies
and look at the services

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.280
that we have to do,
tests and procedures,

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:17.570
and determine if it will
be covered by Medicare.

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:20.950
So as it says Medicare
coverage analysis,

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:23.240
is this going to be
covered by Medicare?

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:28.060
So in 2000, the
Clinton administration

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:30.130
instituted a clinical
trial policy.

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:33.820
Basically, what it
is is that if it

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:38.230
is a qualified clinical trial,
certain items and services

00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:42.070
can be billed to the insurance,
even though it's doing inside

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:44.030
of the clinical trial.

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:48.070
So with that, in all
the research that we do,

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:51.310
we perform Medicare
coverage analysis.

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:55.180
And this is just a small part
of the whole research process

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:57.170
that Allison was talking about.

00:09:57.170 --> 00:10:01.360
And this is where Pluto7
and USC partner took over,

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:03.850
and perhaps that we can
bring machine learning

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:08.810
and AI to streamline and reduce
the time they were spending.

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:14.020
So the process is that we
receive a protocol document,

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:16.090
meaning that's the
study document where

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:20.050
it has study design
and objectives

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:23.470
and what we need to do, what
we are trying to accomplish

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:27.330
through this clinical trial,
and who's going to do what

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:28.960
and what needs to be done.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:31.420
So it's usually a PDF document.

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:32.470
We receive it.

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:35.980
And a coverage
analyst, like myself,

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:37.660
will review the protocol.

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:40.150
And protocols are
usually pretty thick,

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:42.160
and there are a lot of
scientific language.

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:44.470
But what main thing
that we look at

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:48.830
is what's called study calendar,
something that looks like this.

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:51.658
This is just off
from a protocol.

00:10:51.658 --> 00:10:54.890
Set a calendar, schedule
event, we call it.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:57.610
It shows on the left
side what things it needs

00:10:57.610 --> 00:10:59.740
to be done at what time point.

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:04.970
So this is just from a
protocol that's out there.

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:11.100
And so, we review the study
calendar, which is in PDF form.

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:15.730
And, in order for us to do
coverage analysis, at USC,

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:17.380
we use something called Encore.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:21.070
It's a CTMS Clinical
Trial Management System--

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:22.340
it's a software.

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:24.730
We use we do
everything in Encore.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:27.430
However, in many
institutions, if they're not

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:30.550
using Encore or
CTMS, they choose

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:32.770
to do it in Excel spreadsheet.

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:34.810
So, basically, if we
didn't have Encore,

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:38.920
as a person what I had to
do is to read the protocol

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:41.980
and look at this page
and then create--

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:44.750
basically copy and
paste manually.

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:47.170
So I'm typing everything
on the left side

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:50.180
and then creating all the
columns and putting all the Xs.

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:52.630
Xs Just mean it's
happening at this point.

00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:59.990
So, that's the first
step-- basically creating

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:03.290
an Excel version for all
this that is to be done.

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:07.070
At this point, I'm not doing any
analysis, I'm just copying it.

00:12:07.070 --> 00:12:09.800
And this is where
Pluto7 came in,

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:15.440
and they were able to extract
this information into Excel

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:17.900
spreadsheet by
machine-learning and AI.

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:20.120
And Druva is going to
explain the details

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:22.440
of a technicality of that.

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:27.920
So, once that's done,
what we do first

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:31.520
is we have to find out if it's
a qualified clinical trial--

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:34.680
QCT stands for Qualified
Clinical Trial.

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:36.320
So qualified clinical
trials are a set

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:38.832
of questions that
we have to answer.

00:12:38.832 --> 00:12:40.290
There are a few
questions answered,

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:43.580
but what's most important is
the three questions that all

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:45.200
have to be answered, yes.

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:48.860
The first one is, does this--

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:50.900
we call it IP--

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:54.660
IP in clinical trials means
investigational product.

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:57.590
So if the drug is
investigational product--

00:12:57.590 --> 00:12:58.670
brand new drug--

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:00.800
let's say a company
is developing--

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:05.330
does that fall into
Medicare benefit category?

00:13:05.330 --> 00:13:07.580
Medicare benefit
category is just

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:10.640
a list of services
that can be covered

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:13.700
by Medicare-- not clinical
trial just by Medicare.

00:13:13.700 --> 00:13:16.630
Like for my mother
who is on Medicare--

00:13:16.630 --> 00:13:19.340
when she goes to annual
checkup, that's covered.

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:22.610
And where she needs an eye
exam, that's not covered.

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:25.070
So it's like Medicare
benefit category.

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:30.080
So first, we say, does this
help in the Medicare benefit

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:31.160
category?

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:34.700
And, if it is a drug, it's
usually a drug and biologicals,

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:36.020
so I say, yes.

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:38.720
And the next question
is, does this study

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:40.280
have a therapeutic intent?

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:45.830
Meaning, is this that it
going to somehow bring

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:47.690
quality of life or extend--

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:53.340
create some type of a treatment.

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:56.090
And not just the collecting
data, not just to

00:13:56.090 --> 00:13:58.070
see if it has a toxicity--

00:13:58.070 --> 00:14:00.740
so does it have a
therapeutic intent?

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:01.820
Yes.

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:07.730
And then, next question is,
does this diagnose disease?

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:12.170
So, meaning, the study have
toward their breast cancer

00:14:12.170 --> 00:14:15.470
patients or a pulmonary disease.

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:18.320
So if it is, yes,
all three questions

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:19.970
have to be answered, yes.

00:14:19.970 --> 00:14:22.130
And once this is
answered, yes, then there

00:14:22.130 --> 00:14:24.500
is a fourth question
that has to be

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:27.720
answered that's deemed trial.

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:32.690
So, if it is a study, it's
funded by government--

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:39.110
National Institute of Health
or Department of Defense

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:41.510
or any government
agency funding it--

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:43.100
It's automatically deemed.

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:44.990
Or does it have IND--

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:50.672
investigational new drug--
application filed to FDA?

00:14:50.672 --> 00:14:52.130
So, these are the
type of questions

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:55.130
we go through in QCT checklist.

00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:58.160
And then once it says
it's a qualifying trial--

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:00.770
it's a yes or no-- and
the qualifying trial just

00:15:00.770 --> 00:15:03.860
means that if there are
items and services being done

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:08.100
in the trial, even though
it's doing only for the study,

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:10.190
it can be billed
to the Medicare.

00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:15.530
If it does not qualify, those
investigational items cannot be

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:16.760
billed to insurance.

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:21.320
So, for example-- if
I'm in a clinical trial,

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:22.640
I'm unhealthy subject--

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:26.720
and I'm on this clinical
trial, and if I'm not

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:28.370
on a clinical
trial, I'll just go

00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:30.920
to see my doctor once a year.

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:33.120
That can be billed to Medicare.

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:34.940
But if I'm a clinical
trial and they

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:37.130
want to see me every month--

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:39.265
they want to draw blood--

00:15:39.265 --> 00:15:40.640
that's something
that's not going

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:45.000
to be covered by Medicare on a
non-qualified clinical trial.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:46.320
So that's what we do.

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:50.620
Then that question
drives the next part.

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:52.550
And then what the
next part means

00:15:52.550 --> 00:15:56.780
is that when we see this
one, we have to determine,

00:15:56.780 --> 00:16:00.230
for example, very
top informed consent.

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:02.210
That's something
I look at and go,

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:06.770
is this considered
research item or what

00:16:06.770 --> 00:16:09.440
we call standard of care?

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:11.120
Informed consent
just simply means

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:13.250
that patient is
signing a consent

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:16.110
form that is, I want to
be in this clinical trial.

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:19.610
So, if the patient is
not on a clinical trial,

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:22.740
there's no reason for them
to sign informed consent.

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:24.050
So there'll be research.

00:16:24.050 --> 00:16:27.470
But usually if
you go down, let's

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:31.730
say there is a
complete physical exam.

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:34.640
Physical exam is being
done as screening.

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:37.190
And the one below is
abbreviated physical exam

00:16:37.190 --> 00:16:39.930
that's being done at
different time points.

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:42.920
So, at this
different time point,

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:46.190
research, meaning something
other than interest

00:16:46.190 --> 00:16:48.110
is going to pay for it.

00:16:48.110 --> 00:16:51.610
Standard care meaning
it can be billed

00:16:51.610 --> 00:16:53.520
to the Medicare and insurance.

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:57.220
So extracting it,
in this example,

00:16:57.220 --> 00:17:00.060
we say screening for
the physical exam.

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:04.210
At screening very first
visit, we say research.

00:17:04.210 --> 00:17:08.160
But then, you go down and
determine that each one of them

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:11.170
are by the standard
of care research.

00:17:11.170 --> 00:17:14.589
So, that's the process of
Medicare coverage analysis.

00:17:14.589 --> 00:17:16.855
That analyzes each one of them--

00:17:16.855 --> 00:17:18.480
it's not just me just
saying, oh, yeah,

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:19.900
I feel like saying
it's research.

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:21.670
I feel like saying
it's standard care.

00:17:21.670 --> 00:17:25.560
But, we do go by the national
coverage determination.

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:29.070
There is a government reference
that we have to follow,

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:33.830
or there is a national society
guidelines for cancer trials.

00:17:33.830 --> 00:17:36.720
So, we have something
called NCCN guidelines--

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:39.810
National Comprehensive
Cancer Network guidelines--

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:43.080
that tells us, OK, all these
patients in a breast cancer

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:48.840
study to have the image CT
scans done every six months

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:50.400
is something routine.

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:54.340
So, we use that reference point
to make that determination.

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:57.840
So, this whole process takes
from basically somebody

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:00.060
sitting down,
reading the protocol,

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:03.390
extracting it, reading or
finding all the references,

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:05.880
and then making into
this determination.

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:09.390
This is a very lengthy process,
because it's not something

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:13.020
that-- if I don't do
it right, then USC

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:17.680
can be fined, because it's
something that even though I

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:20.610
make maybe a mistake,
but if I did not

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:23.340
do the determination
correctly, if I bill something

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:28.200
to the Medicare incorrectly,
the Medicare what

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:30.900
should be the last
pair, and then I'd say

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:32.500
that you are the first pair.

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:35.620
So, this is our manual
process that we go through.

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:37.680
And then Druva's
going to come and how

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:41.040
the Pluto7 came in
and streamlined while

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:43.950
part of this process
in the streamline.

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:44.773
Thank you.

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:52.870
DRUVA REDDY TIRUVURU: Thank you
so much, Joanne and Allison.

00:18:55.970 --> 00:18:57.380
So, this is how it works.

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:01.820
We have clinical
trials protocols

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:04.100
coming in the form of a PDF.

00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:06.620
They are stored in
Google Cloud Storage.

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:11.060
Google Cloud Storage is a
RESTful online file system web

00:19:11.060 --> 00:19:13.850
service offered by Google
where you can store and access

00:19:13.850 --> 00:19:17.120
your data on GCP infrastructure.

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:20.700
And, followed by that,
we have app engine here.

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:23.360
App engine is a
serverless instance.

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:25.070
It's fully managed.

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:28.100
By fully managed, I mean
that the applications

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:31.490
that are deployed on the App
Engine scales automatically.

00:19:31.490 --> 00:19:33.470
And you don't have
to worry about

00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:35.780
the underlying infrastructure.

00:19:35.780 --> 00:19:39.500
All you have to focus
on is your code.

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:43.670
Oh, here, app engine
access choreographer

00:19:43.670 --> 00:19:46.130
like a Data Manager.

00:19:46.130 --> 00:19:49.430
It follows the data into
the Cloud Vision API.

00:19:49.430 --> 00:19:53.070
Cloud Vision API is a pre-built,
machine-learning model

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:57.740
offered by Google, which can be
accessed through RESTful API.

00:19:57.740 --> 00:20:01.430
It helps us to enable
and detect the text

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:05.270
present in the clinical trial
protocols and extract it.

00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:09.920
And, followed by that, we have
a cloud machine-learning engine,

00:20:09.920 --> 00:20:13.620
where we hosted our
one-day convolution

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:15.830
on your intellectual model.

00:20:15.830 --> 00:20:19.250
It's serverless
and fully managed.

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:23.660
Or you can also custom
select your resources

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:24.800
that you would like.

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:29.090
And it also offers training
and predicting services

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:32.000
individually or together.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:36.950
I have kept the doc
high level, but if you

00:20:36.950 --> 00:20:39.500
have any specific
questions regarding

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:43.010
the technical details, please
feel free to reach out to me.

00:20:43.010 --> 00:20:45.600
Before going to the next
slide, by a show of hands,

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:49.640
how many of you here are data
scientists and data engineers?

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:50.150
Very good.

00:20:50.150 --> 00:20:51.317
That's a pretty good number.

00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:57.940
Let me talk about the data set.

00:20:57.940 --> 00:21:02.600
The data set that we use here
are from two different sources.

00:21:02.600 --> 00:21:07.420
One is USC registry that the
historical clinical trial

00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:09.880
protocols they had
over the years.

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:14.650
And followed by that, we
had publicly available data

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:19.280
sets that we got from
clinicaltrial.gov.

00:21:19.280 --> 00:21:22.300
It's United States
Library of Medicine--

00:21:22.300 --> 00:21:24.070
they're offering this.

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:28.910
And we tagged clinical trial
statements extracted from

00:21:28.910 --> 00:21:31.990
40,000-plus clinical
trial protocols.

00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:36.560
I feel that this data set is
really good as it was written,

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:38.890
the clinical trial terms.

00:21:38.890 --> 00:21:42.610
And it's publicly available
in the URL mentioned here.

00:21:46.640 --> 00:21:52.300
So, on your left, you
can see a snapshot of one

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:55.570
of the pages of the clinical
trial protocol which

00:21:55.570 --> 00:21:58.780
has the study objectives,
primary objectives,

00:21:58.780 --> 00:22:01.280
secondary objectives, and so on.

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:03.370
We are passing the
clinical trial protocols

00:22:03.370 --> 00:22:06.675
in the form of a PDF into
Cloud Vision API, which

00:22:06.675 --> 00:22:10.350
is a pre-built machine-learning
model that I mentioned before.

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:13.780
It has multiple features
like label detection,

00:22:13.780 --> 00:22:16.360
logo detection,
face recognition,

00:22:16.360 --> 00:22:17.950
handwriting recognition.

00:22:17.950 --> 00:22:21.010
But, in this project, we
leverage optical character

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:23.560
recognition feature
of Cloud Vision

00:22:23.560 --> 00:22:28.900
API where it could
detect and extract

00:22:28.900 --> 00:22:30.250
the text out of the PDFs.

00:22:34.600 --> 00:22:38.780
And followed by that,
text pre-processing

00:22:38.780 --> 00:22:42.260
is an integral part, an
extremely important step

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:44.240
of machine-learning journey.

00:22:47.130 --> 00:22:49.850
We spend a significant
amount of time

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:54.650
there, because the
quality of data

00:22:54.650 --> 00:22:58.550
and the amount of information
that you can extract out

00:22:58.550 --> 00:23:00.620
of the data is
extremely important,

00:23:00.620 --> 00:23:04.830
and it determines the ability
of your quality of the model.

00:23:04.830 --> 00:23:06.740
And we spend a
significant amount of time

00:23:06.740 --> 00:23:08.900
there in pre-processing.

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:14.120
As part of pre-processing, we
transformed all the statements

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:16.820
that were extracted
by Cloud Vision API

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:20.570
into a sequence of words
separated by a whitespace.

00:23:20.570 --> 00:23:24.530
And then we also had to
remove the punctuation,

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:26.690
special characteristics--

00:23:26.690 --> 00:23:30.110
characters like
non-alpha numeric symbols

00:23:30.110 --> 00:23:35.000
like question marks, or
the Greek signs, et cetera.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:38.030
And then once we
pre-process the data,

00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:41.540
we found that the data
was highly unbalanced.

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:47.270
The data is called unbalanced
when a class of data

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:49.420
has a huge dominance
over the other.

00:23:49.420 --> 00:23:54.250
Over here, 70% of the
statements that were extracted

00:23:54.250 --> 00:23:57.890
were labeled as research,
like Joanne was mentioning.

00:23:57.890 --> 00:24:00.500
Research is something that
Medicare doesn't cover,

00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:03.230
whereas standard is.

00:24:03.230 --> 00:24:07.340
We had standard
statements to only 30%

00:24:07.340 --> 00:24:11.540
of the entire statements
that were extracted.

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:18.800
To overcome that, we
used a random, unbalanced

00:24:18.800 --> 00:24:20.180
undersampling.

00:24:20.180 --> 00:24:22.080
What does that mean?

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:27.530
We take the classes from
the minority classes

00:24:27.530 --> 00:24:32.810
and match it against the
majority so that they are equal

00:24:32.810 --> 00:24:37.670
and use the rest of the
classes during validation.

00:24:37.670 --> 00:24:40.970
Followed by that,
we did training.

00:24:40.970 --> 00:24:45.230
We used an open source
library called FastText.

00:24:45.230 --> 00:24:49.490
It's used for text
representation and then text

00:24:49.490 --> 00:24:53.400
classification and
workbook representation.

00:24:53.400 --> 00:24:55.830
FastText is an
extension of Word2Vec.

00:24:55.830 --> 00:25:00.650
It's a predictor model that
uses raw text as input,

00:25:00.650 --> 00:25:05.060
and it predicts the word
given its surrounding

00:25:05.060 --> 00:25:06.650
in the context and vice versa.

00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:14.010
Over here, once we did
the embedded training,

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:19.110
we tested it out using
a very rare word, which

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:21.480
is gastroenteritis.

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:24.690
Thanks to Joanne, this is how
we will work with the customers

00:25:24.690 --> 00:25:27.120
to understand their
data, and that's

00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:29.700
where their domain
expertise comes in.

00:25:29.700 --> 00:25:31.590
It's a very rare data.

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:33.560
And, as you can see,
it could figure out

00:25:33.560 --> 00:25:38.940
the corresponding embeddings
for that particular word.

00:25:38.940 --> 00:25:41.560
Here, it didn't do when it
appeared in the training data,

00:25:41.560 --> 00:25:45.150
but the FastText
embedder training

00:25:45.150 --> 00:25:48.510
could figure out
the word endings,

00:25:48.510 --> 00:25:51.650
whereas when we tried out
other open source libraries,

00:25:51.650 --> 00:25:56.730
it throwed in error, since that
gastroenteritis word wasn't

00:25:56.730 --> 00:26:01.890
present in the training calls.

00:26:01.890 --> 00:26:03.720
By the way, if
anyone is wondering

00:26:03.720 --> 00:26:08.960
what the gastroenteritis
is-- it's a type of a flu.

00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:11.910
Joanne helped me to
understand what that is.

00:26:11.910 --> 00:26:15.120
So you put FastText word
embedding could also

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:17.610
figure out, it could generate
better word embeddings

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:18.660
for rare words.

00:26:18.660 --> 00:26:21.630
And, also, it could
find word embeddings

00:26:21.630 --> 00:26:25.675
for the words which are not even
in the part of the vocabulary.

00:26:28.590 --> 00:26:32.520
And, as part of the next
step, our text classifier

00:26:32.520 --> 00:26:35.640
was trained using
deep neural networks,

00:26:35.640 --> 00:26:38.720
with preordained
embeddings that we

00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:41.000
built in the previous
steps as inputs

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:44.510
to predict whether or not the
short free text statements that

00:26:44.510 --> 00:26:47.525
are extracted using
Cloud Vision API.

00:26:50.290 --> 00:26:52.450
This is standard-- or
that means whether it's

00:26:52.450 --> 00:26:56.960
covered by Medicare or
it's covered by research.

00:26:56.960 --> 00:26:58.520
So, the pre-trained
word embeddings

00:26:58.520 --> 00:27:03.325
were used as input for 1D
convolutional neural networks.

00:27:03.325 --> 00:27:09.440
And we hosted that onto a Google
Cloud machine-learning engine.

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:13.760
So those three
points are the steps

00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:17.540
that we have taken
before deploying

00:27:17.540 --> 00:27:20.510
our model on the GCM--

00:27:20.510 --> 00:27:22.310
just converting all
the standard sentences

00:27:22.310 --> 00:27:25.640
into a data set in the
sequence of word indexes

00:27:25.640 --> 00:27:29.110
and reshuffle the
training and test data

00:27:29.110 --> 00:27:32.100
sets, and also prepare
an embeddings matrix,

00:27:32.100 --> 00:27:37.580
which contain an index I. The
embedding vector for the word

00:27:37.580 --> 00:27:41.980
from the index I.

00:27:41.980 --> 00:27:46.010
And this is the
overall model where

00:27:46.010 --> 00:27:49.830
the input text, which is
pre-processsed and labeled,

00:27:49.830 --> 00:27:53.960
is taken and we did an embedding
training of FastText, which

00:27:53.960 --> 00:27:55.890
is an extension of Word2Vec.

00:27:55.890 --> 00:28:01.320
And then we trained classify
using 1D convolution neural

00:28:01.320 --> 00:28:01.990
network.

00:28:01.990 --> 00:28:05.580
And we got the predictions
where the particular statement

00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:07.575
is research or standard.

00:28:11.110 --> 00:28:12.770
So these are all the results.

00:28:12.770 --> 00:28:17.530
Our model could predict whether
that statement is research

00:28:17.530 --> 00:28:21.550
or standard with 86% accuracy.

00:28:21.550 --> 00:28:26.800
And the other advantages of
the model that we have is it's

00:28:26.800 --> 00:28:29.530
quick to train on GPU.

00:28:29.530 --> 00:28:32.110
So, within 10 to
15 minutes, we were

00:28:32.110 --> 00:28:35.100
better able to train
our model and then

00:28:35.100 --> 00:28:39.310
we could try out
multiple architectures

00:28:39.310 --> 00:28:41.470
within a short amount of time.

00:28:41.470 --> 00:28:45.940
And the other one is
UCPR in French time,

00:28:45.940 --> 00:28:47.800
once your model
is up and running.

00:28:47.800 --> 00:28:50.840
You don't need a GPU,
can do it on CPU,

00:28:50.840 --> 00:28:53.620
which is lower cost
compared to GPU.

00:28:53.620 --> 00:28:56.750
And third point is you can
train periodically if required.

00:28:56.750 --> 00:28:59.230
Consider if a new
set of protocols

00:28:59.230 --> 00:29:03.400
comes into their registry and
the guidelines keep changing,

00:29:03.400 --> 00:29:05.600
new drugs come in--

00:29:05.600 --> 00:29:07.630
so you can keep
your model updated

00:29:07.630 --> 00:29:13.300
by retraining it
periodically if necessary.

00:29:13.300 --> 00:29:18.100
And so I'll pass
it on to Allison.

00:29:18.100 --> 00:29:20.130
ALLISON ORECHWA: Just,
in terms of closing,

00:29:20.130 --> 00:29:22.470
from USC's
perspective, we really

00:29:22.470 --> 00:29:25.970
felt like this was a successful
proof of concept project.

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:28.470
So, the concept has been proven.

00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:33.570
Compared to a human-only
determination process,

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:36.000
this technology really showed
that it could drastically

00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:39.750
reduce the time needed for
this part of the processing.

00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:44.760
So, we hope that we can extend
the solution beyond breast

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:48.360
